# document_store — binary write-path containment [SPEC:CXC-DOCSTORE]

> This is a NARROW node, scoped to one invariant, written alongside the code that satisfies it
> (`DEFECT-DOCUMENT-STORE-WRITE-PATH-PUT-D8CCDE`). It is not a conversion of `spec/`-side prose —
> document_store has none describing this surface — and it does not attempt to specify the rest of
> the service. It exists so this specific behavior is never again code-only: [CXC-BUSSTORE-CONTAINMENT]
> already proved that a containment guard living in exactly one handler is a guard the other handlers
> cannot reach, and a guard living in code with no spec node is the same failure one level up — the
> NEXT reimplementation of this service has nothing but the source to tell it the guard is load-bearing.

---

### [CXC-DOCSTORE-CONTAINMENT] Every request-supplied blob id resolves INSIDE `blobs/` — one primitive, every call site

- invariant: `collection` and `doc_id`, both request-supplied, are turned into a filesystem path by exactly one primitive, `_resolve_contained(root, *parts)`, local to `services/document_store/server.py`. Every handler that builds a blob path (`_blob_path`, reached by `put_binary`, `get_binary`, `delete_binary`) calls it. A resolved path outside `root` (`blobs/`) is **403**; a segment that cannot be resolved at all (an embedded NUL) is **400**. No route is exempt and no segment is trusted because of where it came from.
- write-path-was-the-hole: `put_binary` wrote the blob to disk (`os.makedirs` + atomic write) BEFORE any database gate — there was no metadata lookup standing between an attacker-controlled `doc_id` and a filesystem write. `get_binary` and `delete_binary` were structurally safer (both call `get_binary_meta`, a DB lookup, FIRST, and return `None`/`False` on a miss) but are guarded uniformly here for defense-in-depth and because a DB-first check is an accident of call order, not a designed containment boundary.
- three-forms-three-guards: same three attack shapes as [CXC-BUSSTORE-CONTAINMENT] `three-forms-three-guards`, because the underlying trap is the same Python behavior, not something particular to bus_storage — `../` directly; `%2e%2e/`, defeated because `_route` calls `unquote(self.path)` before any segment is extracted, so the wire-form string never reaches the guard un-decoded; and a LEADING SLASH on either `collection` or `doc_id`, which `os.path.join(root, "/etc/passwd")` silently resolves to `/etc/passwd` — discarding `root` — with no `..` anywhere in the request for a substring check to catch.
- resolve-do-not-grep: containment is decided by `os.path.realpath` and an ancestry check (`target == root_real or target.startswith(root_real + os.sep)`), never by string-matching for `..`.
- positive-control-is-part-of-the-invariant: `doc_id` LEGITIMATELY contains `/` — nested ids such as `a/b/c.bin` are a normal, supported shape and MUST continue to resolve. Containment is computed against the `blobs/` root as a whole, not per-collection, precisely so a nested id is not mistaken for an escape. A guard that rejects `nested/a/b.bin` has traded a vulnerability for breaking every caller storing structured blob ids.
- not-shared-with-bus_storage: `_resolve_contained` is defined LOCALLY in `services/document_store/server.py`, a deliberate copy of the bus_storage primitive of the same name and contract, not an import — the two services are separate deploy units and importing across them would couple two independently-deployed processes for a ~25-line function. Keep both in sync by hand if the shared contract changes; a future de-fork into one importable module is a separate decision, not implied here.
- json-path-is-out-of-scope-by-construction: `_store.put` / `.get` / `.delete` (the JSON document path) are SQLite-keyed — `collection` and `doc_id` are query parameters, never filesystem path components — so they carry no equivalent hole and need no guard.
- enforced-by: `tests/test_document_store.py::TestBinaryPathEscape`, which PUTs a binary with a `../`-laden `doc_id` and a `%2e%2e`-encoded variant, asserts each is refused 4xx AND that no file was created outside `blobs/` (the pre-fix target path, computed the same way `_blob_path` used to), paired with a positive control (`nested/a/b.bin` PUTs and round-trips) so the guard is proven not to have broken legitimate nested ids.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-DOCSTORE]

